[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Unity DOTS: ECS, Burst, and Job System"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Data-Oriented Technology Stack (DOTS) in Unity is a high-performance framework designed to maximize modern multi-core CPU architectures. By decoupling data from behavior and leveraging cache locality, DOTS allows developers to simulate thousands of entities simultaneously at high framerates."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Pillars of DOTS"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Entity Component System (ECS): The architectural pattern that organizes data into contiguous memory layouts."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"C# Job System: Safely executes multi-threaded code without race conditions."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Burst Compiler: A LLVM-based compiler that translates C# jobs into highly optimized machine code."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Defining Components"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In ECS, data is stored in structs implementing IComponentData instead of Monobehaviours. Here is how you define a simple rotation speed component."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"using Unity.Entities;

public struct RotationSpeedData : IComponentData
{
    public float RadiansPerSecond;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Creating Systems"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Systems contain the logic that operates on entities matching specific component queries. We use partial struct definitions and the BurstCompiler for optimization."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Mathematics;
using Unity.Burst;

public partial struct RotationSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        foreach (var (transform, speed) in SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<LocalTransform>, RefRO<RotationSpeedData>>())
        {
            transform.ValueRW = transform.ValueRO.RotateY(speed.ValueRO.RadiansPerSecond * deltaTime);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Leveraging the Job System"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To scale operations across multiple CPU cores, we can implement IJobEntity, which allows the Burst compiler to vectorize and parallelize the work effortlessly."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Mathematics;
using Unity.Burst;

[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ParallelRotationSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        new RotateJob
        {
            DeltaTime = deltaTime
        }.ScheduleParallel();
    }
}

[BurstCompile]
public partial struct RotateJob : IJobEntity
{
    public float DeltaTime;

    public void Execute(ref LocalTransform transform, in RotationSpeedData speed)
    {
        transform = transform.RotateY(speed.RadiansPerSecond * DeltaTime);
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining ECS, the Job System, and the Burst Compiler, Unity developers can bypass the traditional garbage collection and cache-miss bottlenecks of standard MonoBehaviours. Practice converting simple gameplay mechanics into IComponentData and IJobEntity to master data-oriented design."}]
